      "text": "To find the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\), we first perform the division to determine the decimal expansion.\n\nCalculating \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n& 350 + 230 + 100 + 80 + 50 + 40 + 30 + 20 + 10 + 7 = 3125 + 3000 + 1750 + 850 + 400 + 350 + 270 + 160 + 90 + 4 = 6445.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe round down and get the quotient:\n\n\\[\n\\left[\\dfrac{5}{14}\\right] = 0.3571428571428571\\ldots\n\\]\n\nIn the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\), we observe the repeating sequence: \\(3571428571428571\\ldots\\) which repeats every 6 figures.\n\nTo find the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit, we need to count how many complete cycles of the repeating sequence fit into \\(1314\\) digits. We start by determining the division of 1314 by 6.\n\nExecuting \\(1314 \\div 6\\), we determine the quotient:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1314 \\div 6 &= 219 \\text{ remainder } 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe remainder \\(0\\) indicates that \\(1314\\) digits split evenly into the repeating sequence. Thus, the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit is the same as the first digit in the sequence \\(357142\\).\n\nTo check if the sequence repeats, let's count the digits:\n\n- The first 6 digits are \\(357142\\),\n- The next 6 digits can be considered part of the same sequence.\n\nThus, \\(1314 - 1 (\\text{the first digit}) = 1313 \\text{ more digits}\\rightarrow\\) complete 219 cycles and \\(3 \\text{ incomplete digits}\\).\n\nFinally to find \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit multiply 3 digits each of 6 repeats:\n\n\\[\n3 \\times 6 = 18\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder after complete cycles, plus the starting digit gives:\n\n\\[\n219 \\times 1 = 219, 18 + 1 = 220\n\\]\n\nwhich is an odd number leading to the first digit of \\(357142\\).\n\nThus, the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
